---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

// Change this if a different address should receive privacy mail.
const contact = 'privacy@bikes.pizza';
const updated = 'September 9, 2026';
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy Policy · bikes.pizza"
  description="What bikes.pizza collects, why, and how to have it deleted."
>
  <div class="container mx-auto px-6 py-16 max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-light tracking-wide text-ink">Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted text-sm font-light">Last updated {updated}</p>

    <div class="mt-12 space-y-10 text-ink font-light leading-relaxed">
      <section>
        <p>
          bikes.pizza is a place to share photographs of bicycles and pizza. This
          policy covers the bikes.pizza website and the bikes.pizza apps for iOS
          and Android, all operated by Pizza Predator LLC.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          The short version: we collect what an account and a photo submission
          need, and nothing else. There is no analytics SDK, no advertising, no
          third-party tracker, and no profile built about you. We do not sell
          personal information, and we never have.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light text-ink">What we collect</h2>

        <h3 class="mt-6 text-lg text-ink">Your account</h3>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Signing up stores your email address and an account identifier. You can
          sign in with an email address and password, with Google, or with Apple.
          When you sign in with Google or Apple we receive your email address from
          them; if you use Apple's Hide My Email, we only ever see the relay
          address Apple gives us, not your real one.
        </p>

        <h3 class="mt-6 text-lg text-ink">Passkeys and two-factor codes</h3>
        <p class="mt-2">
          If you create a passkey we store its public key, its credential
          identifier, and when it was created and last used. The private key stays
          on your device or in your password manager and is never sent to us — it
          is not something we can read, copy, or hand over. If you enroll an
          authenticator app instead, we store the shared secret that verifies your
          codes.
        </p>

        <h3 class="mt-6 text-lg text-ink">Submissions</h3>
        <p class="mt-2">
          When you submit a photo we store the image together with the title,
          description, and name you attach to it, plus the account it came from
          and the time it arrived. Photos you submit are intended for publication:
          if a reviewer approves one, it appears publicly on bikes.pizza with the
          title, description, and name you supplied. Your email address is not
          published.
        </p>

        <h3 class="mt-6 text-lg text-ink">On your device</h3>
        <p class="mt-2">
          The app and the website keep a little information on your own device —
          whether you are signed in, your light or dark theme choice, and what is
          in your shopping cart. This stays on the device and is not a tracking
          cookie.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light text-ink">What we do not collect</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          We do not collect your location, your contacts, your health or fitness
          data, your browsing on other sites, or an advertising identifier. The app
          asks for camera and photo library access only so that you can choose a
          picture to submit; it does not read your photo library otherwise, and
          nothing is uploaded until you submit it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light text-ink">How submitted photos are reviewed</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Every submitted photo is checked automatically by Google Cloud Vision
          before anyone looks at it. That check screens for unsafe content and for
          pictures containing people or faces, which this site does not accept. A
          human reviewer then approves, queues, or rejects the submission. The
          automated check is a filter, not a decision about you, and it is not used
          to identify anyone.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light text-ink">Who else processes your data</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          We use a small number of service providers, each handling only what its
          job requires:
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 list-disc list-outside pl-6">
          <li><span class="text-ink">Google Firebase</span> — accounts and sign-in, the database, file storage for photos, and the server functions behind the app.</li>
          <li><span class="text-ink">Google Cloud Vision</span> — the automated photo check described above.</li>
          <li><span class="text-ink">Sanity</span> — stores the posts that have been published to the site.</li>
          <li><span class="text-ink">Mailgun</span> — sends the notification email that tells a reviewer a submission is waiting.</li>
          <li><span class="text-ink">Shopify</span> — runs the shop. If you buy something, your order and payment details are handled by Shopify under their privacy policy; we never see your card number.</li>
          <li><span class="text-ink">Cloudflare</span> — serves the website and its domain.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light text-ink">Deleting your account</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          You can delete your account yourself, at any time, from the Settings
          screen in the app or from the account page on this website. Deleting an
          account removes your sign-in details, your email address, and your
          passkeys.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          Photos that have already been published as posts stay on the site, since
          they are part of the public record of what has been posted. If you would
          like a published photo taken down as well, write to us and we will remove
          it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light text-ink">Keeping it</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Account information is kept for as long as the account exists. Rejected
          submissions are kept only long enough to handle any question about the
          decision. Published posts are kept until they are taken down.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light text-ink">Children</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          bikes.pizza is not directed at children under 13, and we do not knowingly
          collect information from them. If you believe a child has created an
          account, write to us and we will remove it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light text-ink">Your rights</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          You can ask us for a copy of the information we hold about you, ask us to
          correct it, or ask us to delete it. Depending on where you live you may
          have further rights over that information, including under the GDPR or
          the CCPA. Write to the address below and we will help.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light text-ink">Changes</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          If this policy changes we will update the date at the top of this page.
          Substantial changes will be announced on the site.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light text-ink">Contact</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Questions about privacy, or a request about your data, can go to{' '}
          <a href={`mailto:${contact}`} class="text-spice hover:underline">{contact}</a>.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
